      "id": "01",
      "source_unit": "sets",
      "title": "Create a Set and Print It Sorted",
      "sidebar_tag": "set",
      "description": [
        "<p>Read three integers into a set (using curly braces), then print",
        "them in sorted order.</p>",
        "<pre>&gt; python program.py",
        "3",
        "1",
        "2",
        "[1, 2, 3]</pre>",
        "<p>A set looks like a list or tuple, but uses curly braces:",
        "<code>{a, b, c}</code>. Unlike a list, a set never keeps duplicate",
        "values, and it doesn't remember any particular order &mdash; so it's",
        "common to sort a set with <code>sorted()</code> before printing it, to",
        "get consistent, readable output.</p>"
      ],
      "starter": [
        "a = int(input())",
        "b = int(input())",
        "c = int(input())",
        "",
        "# TODO: create a set from a, b, c, then print sorted(that_set)"
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "stdin": [
            "1",
            "2",
            "3"
          ],
          "expected": "[1, 2, 3]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "5",
            "5",
            "5"
          ],
          "expected": "[5]"
        },
        {
          "stdin": [
            "3",
            "1",
            "2"
          ],
          "expected": "[1, 2, 3]"
        }
      ]
    },
